course: dictionary
lesson: 6552
title: Who reads the news?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A newsreader is the person you see or hear reading the news on television or radio. Look at the context: «Newsreaders speak slowly and look serious about deaths to show respect for the people who died and their families.» The clues are speak slowly, look serious and respect, and they all point to a person who reads the news.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Put in "people who read the news on TV" instead of newsreaders: "People who read the news on TV speak slowly and look serious about deaths." It still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
explain: In the USA, people often say news anchor or newscaster for the same job. If you hear those words, they mean the same as newsreader.
fact: A newsreader is a person who reads the news on television or radio.
fact: «Newsreader» is the word used in the UK and Australia.
fact: In the USA, the usual words are «news anchor» or «newscaster».
fact: Newsreaders speak slowly and look serious about deaths to show respect for the people who died and their families.
exercise: «Newsreaders speak slowly and look serious about deaths.» What is a newsreader? | A person who writes books for children | A person who sells newspapers in a shop | *A person who reads the news on television or radio | A person who fixes televisions
exercise: Which word is often used in the USA for a newsreader? | *news anchor | newsagent | nurse | chef
exercise: Why do newsreaders look serious about deaths? | To make people laugh | Because they are bored | Because the news is always funny | *To show respect for the people who died and their families
test: Ben watches the evening news on TV. Who is reading it to him? | A gardener | A dentist | *A newsreader | A taxi driver
test: What does a newsreader do? | *Reads the news on television or radio | Mends roads and bridges | Sells bread in a bakery | Drives buses to the station
exam: Why is it useful to swap your guess into the sentence? | To make the sentence longer | To avoid reading the rest of the text | *To check that your meaning still makes sense | To find a word that rhymes
